<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
<p class="font-bold">A fair coin creates either one waking or two indistinguishable wakings.</p>
<div class="grid grid-cols-[5rem_1fr] gap-x-4 gap-y-3 items-center text-base"><p class="font-bold">Heads</p><div class="border-l-4 border-[#387a91] pl-4"><p>Monday: wakes</p><p class="text-sm">No Tuesday waking</p></div><p class="font-bold">Tails</p><div class="border-l-4 border-[#bd4b37] pl-4"><p>Monday: wakes</p><p>Tuesday: wakes again, with memory erased</p></div></div>
<div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-base"><div class="border border-ink p-3"><strong>Halfers</strong><p>Heads = 1/2</p><p>Waking gives no new information about the toss.</p></div><div class="border border-ink p-3"><strong>Thirders</strong><p>Heads = 1/3</p><p>Count the possible wakings.</p></div></div>
<figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">The two coin outcomes have equal initial probability, but tails produces twice as many wakings. Whether the right question counts experiments or indistinguishable waking experiences is precisely the dispute. The picture does not resolve it.</figcaption></figure>